<div class="herocard"><p class="kick">Study skills, plainly put</p>
<h1>Revise so it sticks. Write what was asked.</h1>
<p class="lead">Mentora is a study-skills site about the two things that decide most results: whether your revision produces recall rather than recognition, and whether your essay answers the question that was actually set.</p>
<p class="row"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Use the three tools</a> <a class="btn gh" href="exam-revision-guide.html">Revision guide</a></p></div>
</div></section>
<div class="wrap"><div class="statbar">
<div><b>3</b><span>free tools, no sign-up</span></div>
<div><b>2</b><span>long guides</span></div>
<div><b>4</b><span>referencing styles</span></div>
<div><b>0</b><span>paid placements</span></div>
</div></div>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="two">
<div><p class="kick">What is here</p><h2>Three questions, answered properly</h2>
<ul class="tick">
<li><b>What do I actually need in the final?</b> Weighting, marks already secured and a target, worked out instead of guessed.</li>
<li><b>How do I split the weeks I have left?</b> Hours per subject, sessions a week, and three phases with real dates behind them.</li>
<li><b>How does this reference go?</b> The list entry and the in-text form, side by side, in four styles.</li>
<li><b>Why did a competent essay get a middling mark?</b> Usually because it answered a near-miss version of the question.</li>
<li><b>Why does revision feel like it is working when it is not?</b> Because familiarity is easy to mistake for knowledge.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="quotebox"><p>&ldquo;The mark is decided by what you can produce under pressure, with the book closed and the clock running. Everything in revision should be pointed at that.&rdquo;</p>
<span>The approach behind everything here</span>
<p style="margin:1.2em 0 0;font-size:.95rem;font-family:'Scada',sans-serif;color:#C7CBD5">Nothing on this site is sponsored, and no course, tutor or publisher can pay to be mentioned.</p></div>
</div></div></section>
<section class="tools" id="tools"><div class="wrap">
<p class="kick">Free tools</p><h2 style="margin-bottom:1.6em">Three tools that run in your browser</h2>
<div class="trow" id="grade"><div class="tdesc"><h3>Grade Target Calculator</h3><p>What the remaining assessment has to come back at for you to finish where you want to.</p>
<div class="fgrid"><div class="fld"><label for="gdWeight">Done so far (% of module)</label><input id="gdWeight" type="number" min="1" max="99" value="40"></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="gdGot">Average mark so far (%)</label><input id="gdGot" type="number" min="0" max="100" value="58"></div></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="gdTarget">Target overall (%)</label><input id="gdTarget" type="number" min="1" max="100" value="65"></div></div>
<div class="out" id="gdOut"></div></div>
<div class="trow" id="count"><div class="tdesc"><h3>Exam Countdown Planner</h3><p>Your weeks and hours divided across subjects, in three phases, with an honest word about the load.</p>
<div class="fld"><label for="cdWeeks">Weeks until the first exam</label><input id="cdWeeks" type="number" min="1" max="52" value="8"></div>
<div class="fgrid"><div class="fld"><label for="cdSubs">Subjects</label><input id="cdSubs" type="number" min="1" max="12" value="4"></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="cdHours">Hours a week</label><input id="cdHours" type="number" min="1" max="70" value="14"></div></div></div>
<div class="out" id="cdOut"></div></div>
<div class="trow" id="cite"><div class="tdesc"><h3>Citation Formatter</h3><p>One source, four styles, with the in-text form you will actually need while writing.</p>
<div class="fld"><label for="ctStyle">Style</label><select id="ctStyle"><option value="apa">APA 7</option><option value="mla">MLA 9</option><option value="harvard">Harvard</option><option value="chicago">Chicago notes</option></select></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="ctAuthor">Author</label><input id="ctAuthor" type="text" value="Nadia Okonkwo"></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="ctTitle">Title</label><input id="ctTitle" type="text" value="Reading, Recall and the Revision Myth"></div>
<div class="fgrid"><div class="fld"><label for="ctYear">Year</label><input id="ctYear" type="text" value="2024"></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="ctPub">Publisher</label><input id="ctPub" type="text" value="Fenwick Press"></div></div></div>
<div class="out" id="ctOut"></div></div>
</div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><p class="kick">Long reads</p><h2 style="margin-bottom:1.5em">Two guides, start to finish</h2>
<div class="gtwo">
<article class="gc"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1676276374803-36e48196d5ac?auto=format&fit=crop&w=760&q=75" alt="A whiteboard covered in sticky notes" width="760" height="428" loading="lazy">
<div class="in"><h3>The Exam Revision Guide</h3><p>Why rereading fails and testing works, how to space and interleave without a complicated system, condensing notes down to something you can actually revise from, and what the last fortnight should look like.</p>
<p><a class="btn gh" href="exam-revision-guide.html">Read the guide</a></p></div></article>
<article class="gc"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1607703703674-df96af81dffa?auto=format&fit=crop&w=760&q=75" alt="A hand writing on a whiteboard with a marker" width="760" height="428" loading="lazy">
<div class="in"><h3>The Essay Writing Guide</h3><p>Reading the question properly, building an argument instead of a summary, paragraph structure that a marker can follow, using sources rather than listing them, and editing down to the word count.</p>
<p><a class="btn gh" href="essay-writing-guide.html">Read the guide</a></p></div></article>
</div></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><p class="kick">Questions</p><h2 style="margin-bottom:1.5em">Asked before every exam season</h2>
<div class="qs"><div><h3>Does rereading my notes count as revision?</h3><p>Not really. Rereading feels productive because the material gets easier to recognise, but recognition is not recall. The test is whether you can shut the book and reproduce the argument. If you cannot, you have not learned it yet, however familiar the page looks.</p></div><div><h3>How far ahead should I start revising?</h3><p>Far enough that you can cover every topic once before you start testing yourself, which for most courses means six to eight weeks. The countdown planner splits whatever time you have into covering the ground, testing yourself and sitting full papers.</p></div><div><h3>Is it better to study one subject a day or mix them?</h3><p>Mix them. Switching between related topics in one session is harder while you do it and produces noticeably better recall weeks later. One subject a day feels smoother and fades faster.</p></div><div><h3>What actually loses marks in an essay?</h3><p>Not answering the question. Most essays that come back with a disappointing mark are perfectly competent pieces of writing about a slightly different question than the one set. Underline the command word, write the argument in one sentence, and check every paragraph against it.</p></div><div><h3>How many references does an essay need?</h3><p>There is no universal number, and anyone who gives you one is guessing. What markers look for is whether each claim that needs support has it. A short essay with eight well-used sources beats a long one with thirty that are never discussed.</p></div><div><h3>Do I need to memorise the referencing style?</h3><p>No. Learn the shape of one style well enough to spot mistakes, and look the details up every time. Even experienced academics check. The citation formatter here shows the entry and the in-text form side by side.</p></div></div></div></section>
